course: technology
lesson: 29
title: Tunnels and what lies under the street
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Under the street you walk on there is a hidden city: water pipes, sewers, gas pipes, electricity and internet cables, and sometimes whole railway tunnels. When the road is dug up, you can see a slice of it.
explain: The coloured paint marks that workers spray on the pavement before roadworks are a map of that hidden city, showing where pipes and cables run, so the diggers do not hit them.
explain: Big tunnels today are dug by tunnel-boring machines, giant metal worms with a spinning cutting wheel at the front. As the machine creeps forward, it fits curved concrete pieces into rings behind it, so the tunnel is lined like a pipe as it goes.
explain: Long ago tunnels were dug by hand with picks, shovels and gunpowder by the light of candles and lamps. Old mines and tunnels are still dangerous: they can collapse or be full of bad air, so nobody goes into them for fun.
fact: A tunnel-boring machine grinds through rock or soil with a giant rotating cutting wheel and lines the tunnel with concrete rings behind it; lasers and measurements guide it the right way.
fact: Tunnel-boring machines are often given women's names, from an old tradition linked to Saint Barbara, the saint of miners.
fact: The Channel Tunnel joins England and France under the sea with three tunnels, two for trains and one service tunnel between them; it is about 50 km long, opened in 1994, is nicknamed the Chunnel, and its trains are electric.
fact: Big fans push fresh air through road and rail tunnels and clear smoke if there is a fire, and long tunnels have emergency exits and safe rooms.
fact: Mines are shafts and tunnels dug for coal, metals or salt; some gold mines in South Africa go more than 3 km deep.
fact: Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels; they can collapse or be full of bad air.
fact: Under every street run water pipes, sewers, gas pipes and electricity and internet cables; workers spray coloured paint to mark them, and diggers check for cables before digging.
fact: Sewers are big underground tunnels that carry dirty water and rain to treatment works; London's main sewers were built in the 1860s by Joseph Bazalgette and helped end the city's outbreaks of cholera, a deadly illness spread by dirty water.
fact: Road tunnels have lights, fans, cameras, emergency phones and radio for warnings; the lights are brighter at the entrance so drivers' eyes can get used to the dark.
fact: Before machines, tunnels were dug by hand with picks, shovels and explosives by the light of candles or lamps; the first tunnel under the River Thames opened in 1843.
fact: The Gotthard Base Tunnel runs deep under the Swiss Alps and, at about 57 km, is the longest railway tunnel in the world.
fact: Tunnel lining is made of big curved concrete pieces fitted together into rings, like a pipe; the rings hold back the rock and soil so the tunnel does not collapse.
exercise: How does a tunnel-boring machine dig? | A giant spinning cutting wheel grinds through rock or soil | It blows the rock away with water jets | It melts the rock with fire | It pushes the soil aside like a snowplough
exercise: How does a tunnel-boring machine know which way to go? | Lasers and careful measurements guide it | The driver looks out of a window in the front | It follows the softest rock it can find | It digs straight and hopes for the best
exercise: Why are tunnel-boring machines often given women's names? | From an old tradition linked to Saint Barbara, the saint of miners | Because only women are allowed to drive them | Because the law says every machine needs a name | Because they are named after the builders' boats
exercise: Which two countries does the Channel Tunnel join? | England and France | England and Ireland | France and Spain | Scotland and Norway
exercise: How many tunnels make up the Channel Tunnel? | 3: two for trains and one service tunnel | 1 big tunnel for trains, cars and lorries | 2: one each way, and nothing else at all | 4: two for trains and two more for cars
exercise: What do the big fans in a tunnel do? | Push fresh air through and clear smoke if there is a fire | Cool the trains' wheels | Blow the trains along faster, like wind in a sail, so they use less power | Keep bats out of the tunnel
exercise: About how deep do some gold mines in South Africa go? | More than 3 km | About 30 metres | About 300 km | About 3 metres
exercise: Why must you never go into an old mine or tunnel? | It can collapse or be full of bad air | It is always full of water | Old mines are owned by dragons in stories | You might find gold and get in trouble
exercise: What do coloured paint marks on the road before roadworks show? | Where pipes and cables run underground | Where the next big pothole is going to appear | Where children are allowed to draw with chalk | Where cars must park while the work goes on
exercise: What does a sewer carry? | Dirty water and rain to treatment works | Clean drinking water from the reservoirs to homes | Gas along big pipes to people's kitchen cookers | Electricity to all the street lights in the town
exercise: Who built London's big sewers in the 1860s? | Joseph Bazalgette | Isaac Newton, the scientist | Elisha Otis, the lift maker | Queen Victoria herself
exercise: Why are the lights brighter at the entrance of a road tunnel? | So drivers' eyes can get used to the dark | To show the tunnel's name from far away down the road | To scare animals away from the tunnel entrance | To save electricity deeper inside the long tunnel
exercise: How were tunnels dug before machines? | By hand, with picks, shovels and explosives, by candlelight | By trained moles pulling carts | By pouring strong acid on the rock until it melted into a soft paste | They were never dug before machines
exercise: When did the first tunnel under the River Thames open? | 1843 | 1943 | 1743 | 1993
exercise: Which is the longest railway tunnel in the world? | The Gotthard Base Tunnel in Switzerland | The Channel Tunnel between England and France | The London Underground's Central line, end to end | A tunnel deep under the Pacific Ocean to Japan
exercise: Why don't modern tunnels collapse? | Rings of curved concrete pieces hold back the rock and soil | The trains push the walls out as they pass | The air inside holds the roof up | The rock under cities never moves
exercise: The Gotthard Base Tunnel is about 57 km long and the Channel Tunnel about 50 km. How much longer is the Gotthard? | About 7 km | About 107 km | About 17 km | About 5 km
test: «found an old mine shaft in the woods, wanna explore it with torches» What is right? | Never: old mines can collapse or be full of bad air | Fine, if you bring torches and spare batteries and a snack for later | Fine, if you tie a rope round a tree | Fine, if you go in the daytime
test: «how do trains breathe under the sea in the chunnel» What is right? | The trains are electric, and big fans push fresh air through the tunnels | Trains hold their breath until France | The sea leaks in fresh air through little holes | They run on diesel and bring air in tanks
test: What would happen if a digger dug up the road without checking the marks? | It could hit a gas pipe, water pipe or cable | Nothing, pipes are always buried much too deep for a digger to reach | The road would fill with sand | The marks would move to a new place
test: In a film, a tunnel machine digs a perfect tunnel with nothing lining the walls behind it. What is wrong in real life? | Real machines fit concrete rings behind them so the tunnel does not collapse | Nothing, rock never falls in | Tunnels are lined only after 100 years | Lining is only for decoration
test: «wow, London's sewers are tiny pipes, right» What is right? | Many are big tunnels, built in the 1860s by Joseph Bazalgette | Yes, all sewers are smaller than a drinking straw, so only water fits | London has no sewers at all | Sewers were built only last year
test: «a tunnel under a river in 1843? rubbish, they had no machines» What is right? | It's true: the first Thames tunnel opened in 1843, dug by hand | Right, it was dug in 1943 | Right, tunnels under rivers are impossible, the water would always pour in | It was dug by robots from France
test: You drive from sunshine into a road tunnel. Why don't you go blind for a second? | The lights are brighter at the entrance to help your eyes adjust | Your eyes don't need any time to adjust | The tunnel walls are painted with special paint that soaks up sunlight all day | Cars switch on night vision in tunnels
test: Which is true about the Channel Tunnel? | It opened in 1994 and is about 50 km long | It opened in 1894 and is about 5 km long | It is a bridge, not a tunnel | Cars drive through it on their own wheels in a road tunnel
test: «they called the tunnel machine Ada, lol why» What is the reason? | Tunnel machines get women's names from a tradition linked to Saint Barbara | They name them after the drivers' cats | It is a law that machines need names | Ada is short for 'all digging always'
test: «there's a fire in the tunnel ahead!» Which built-in parts help? | Fans to clear smoke, emergency exits and safe rooms | Water slides that take people to the outside | Nothing, tunnels have no safety parts | Big windows in the roof to let all the smoke out
test: A tunnel's lining has 20 rings, and each ring is made of 6 curved pieces. How many pieces? | 120 | 26 | 100 | 60
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a tunnel-boring machine? | A rotating cutting wheel excavates the rock or soil, whilst concrete segments are erected in rings behind the machine | The machine melts the rock by the application of heat | The machine displaces the soil laterally without excavation | The machine excavates without any lining being installed
exam: Ascertain the purpose of the coloured markings applied to the carriageway prior to excavation. | They indicate the position of pipes and cables beneath the surface | They indicate the location of future potholes | They designate areas reserved for the parking of vehicles belonging to the workmen | They are applied solely for decorative purposes
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the illumination at the portal of a road tunnel exceeds that within. | The eyes of drivers are thereby permitted to adapt to the reduced light | The name of the tunnel is thereby rendered visible at a distance | Animals are thereby deterred from entering | Electricity is thereby economised at the portal
exam: It is given that the Gotthard Base Tunnel measures approximately 57 kilometres and the Channel Tunnel approximately 50 kilometres. Determine by how much the former exceeds the latter. | Approximately seven kilometres | Approximately one hundred and seven kilometres | Approximately seventeen kilometres | Approximately five kilometres
